cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "dual-nonlinear-glu: making the value branch of a gated layer nonlinear"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - alias: PureTearsDropped
repository-code: "https://github.com/PureTearsDropped/dual-nonlinear-glu"
license: 0BSD
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22851806
abstract: >-
  SwiGLU passes its value branch through unchanged. Replacing that identity
  with a second nonlinearity is worth 3.6-4.5x on a teacher-student
  regression benchmark; which nonlinearity is worth a further 8-9%. The
  gate uses a function that saturates below zero (tanh) and the value a
  function that grows logarithmically below zero (asinh); both are linear
  above zero, C^2 but not C^3, with four scale parameters learned per
  channel. The block's output is the mixed second derivative of an
  elementary potential F(u)G(v), which is not the case for SwiGLU. The
  headline margin is shown to be largely a structural match with a product
  teacher, and two competing explanations are tested and eliminated. Also
  recorded: initialising the residual branch at 1/sqrt(depth) outweighs
  every activation choice measured, and a benchmark checklist for detecting
  tasks that do not need depth.
keywords:
  - activation function
  - gated linear unit
  - SwiGLU
  - residual network
  - benchmark methodology
  - negative results
references:
  - type: software
    title: "relu-tanh-asinh: a conjugate f/g residual layer"
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22847921
